path gui-authoring
kind layer
author ai
group gui
note How a human builds a path in the GUI. The path being read is the one steps go into; Nav's target is the step they hang under (None = top level). Selecting a step sets the target, moving the selection off the path keeps it, and the 'top level' button in the target strip clears it, so a reader can collect several symbols under one step. Every add goes through App::add_step: the Listing's 'add step' (selected lines), '+ step' on Symbols and Xrefs rows (Frame::row_with_action), 'add step' under the Xrefs heading, and '+ step' on an off-path graph node, which hangs under the step it was revealed from as a callee. Adding moves neither the selection nor the target. Rearranging is a drag in the Paths outline: a press grabs a row (StepGrab), travel past GRAB_REACH makes it a drag, and the release drops it onto a row's zone - top quarter before, bottom quarter after, the middle under - through Map::place; steps_moved then carries every slot-keyed thing (views, history, selection, target) to the steps' new slots. The Paths tab's 'new path' form and 'new path from it' (promote at depth 1) make paths. The feature-add-step, feature-choose-target, feature-move-step, feature-new-path and feature-promote-symbol paths follow each gesture. Notes, links, kind and group are written through the command line in the Output view; the GUI has no text editor for them.

